so now we start on a new unit called  counting or combinatorics and it's about  counting things now one of the things  that happens when you're counting is you  typically adding up a bunch of numbers  that you've counted along the way and so  you wind up needing to deal with sums a  lot and so let's start with those  mathematical preliminaries we're going  to look at three kinds of sums  arithmetic sums geometric sums and  harmonic sums all of which come up very  regularly and they will have reasonably  nice formulas that explain what they sum  to let's begin with the simplest ones of  arithmetic sums so there's an example  this supposedly is the kind of problem  that was assigned to children in the  night in the 18th century to keep them  busy in class and the great  mathematician Gauss carl friedrich gauss  you know from magnetism and from  probability theory but also in fact the  inventor of congruence and and the  number theory that we've studied showed  his brilliance as a child prodigy when  he was nine years old supposedly he  noticed that in that chart of numbers  that we just saw there were 30 numbers  and each one was 13 greater than the  previous one the idea being that the  tutor didn't want to go through the  effort of summing everything up  he knew the trick to get the sum quickly  but he kept his students busy for hours  doing that kind of problem I don't know  and that this is a true story or not but  it's a good story so let's go on with it  so in other words what Gauss noticed was  that the numbers on that page looked  like 89 and 89 plus 13 down through the  30th number 89 plus 29 times 13 and then  he saw how to get the sum of a the the  simple expression for the value of this  sum and the logic is that let's call the  first term F and then the next term is F  plus 2 DF plus D or D is 13 and F is 89  next one would be F plus 2 D down to the  end and I'm gonna call the last term L  which is 89 plus 29 times 13 this would  be L minus D or 89 times plus 28 times 3  and let's call that some a we don't know  what it is yet but look very quickly  gonna derive it and one of the standard  tricks to find nice formulas for sums is  to find a arithmetic relation between  the sum and it's like perturbation of  the sum in this case I'm just gonna  write the sum backwards so it's the same  sum a but written where the first term  is last and the last term is first and  now notice what happens when I add up  these two sums I get to weigh of course  but every one of these terms this is an  F plus L this is an F plus D plus L  minus T it's F plus L this last one is  an F plus L every one of these pairwise  some sub sums comes out to be F plus L  and now we have a formula for the whole  series a nice simple formula that a is  equal to the sum of the first term plus  the last term divided by two times the  number of terms by the way the first  term plus the last time divided by two  is maybe is more memorable if you  remember that it's the average term it's  the average size term times the number  of terms and that's how you sum up an  arithmetic sum so we can wrap up with a  familiar example namely the sum of the  integers from 1 to N this is an  arithmetic series starts with 1 and the  D that is the difference from successive  terms is simply 1 1 1 plus 1 1 1 plus 1  plus 1 down to n and according to our  formula it's the first plus the last  over 2 times the number of terms and we  have that familiar formula for the sum  of the first n integers
 

